1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:07,160
You have a folder with hundreds of photos, all named like I M G underscore four eight one seven dot J P G.

2
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:10,355
Renaming them one by one would take an afternoon.

3
00:00:10,355 --> 00:00:14,658
Today we'll do it in about ten seconds, with nine lines of Python.

4
00:00:15,358 --> 00:00:17,563
Here's the folder we're starting with.

5
00:00:17,563 --> 00:00:21,974
Six photos with camera names, and one text file that we don't want to touch.

6
00:00:21,974 --> 00:00:24,295
On the right is where we want to end up.

7
00:00:24,295 --> 00:00:27,719
Clean names, numbered in order, with a lowercase extension.

8
00:00:28,419 --> 00:00:32,535
All you need is Python three, installed on Windows, Mac or Linux.

9
00:00:32,535 --> 00:00:35,005
Everything we use today comes built in.

10
00:00:35,005 --> 00:00:36,018
Here's the plan.

11
00:00:36,018 --> 00:00:41,590
Find the files, decide the new names, preview the result, and only then rename for real.

12
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:46,281
One thing trips up almost every beginner, so let's get it right first.

13
00:00:46,281 --> 00:00:50,328
The script has to run from the folder that contains your photos folder.

14
00:00:50,328 --> 00:00:55,915
On Windows, open that folder in File Explorer, click the address bar, type c m d, and press Enter.

15
00:00:55,915 --> 00:00:57,569
A terminal opens right there.

16
00:00:57,569 --> 00:01:03,269
On a Mac, open Terminal, type c d and a space, then drag the folder into the window and press Enter.

17
00:01:03,969 --> 00:01:08,532
To check you're in the right place, type l s on a Mac, or d i r on Windows.

18
00:01:08,532 --> 00:01:10,966
You should see the photos folder listed.

19
00:01:10,966 --> 00:01:12,426
If you do, you're ready.

20
00:01:13,126 --> 00:01:17,848
Create a file called rename dot p y, in the same place as your photos folder.

21
00:01:17,848 --> 00:01:19,995
First, we import Path from pathlib.

22
00:01:19,995 --> 00:01:23,184
Path is the modern way to work with files in Python.

23
00:01:23,184 --> 00:01:28,335
Then we point it at our folder, and use glob to grab every file ending in dot J P G.

24
00:01:28,335 --> 00:01:31,524
We wrap that in sorted, so the order is predictable.

25
00:01:32,224 --> 00:01:33,999
Notice what glob does here.

26
00:01:33,999 --> 00:01:41,627
The star means anything, so star dot J P G matches every file with that ending, and quietly ignores notes dot t x t.

27
00:01:41,627 --> 00:01:42,810
One small warning.

28
00:01:42,810 --> 00:01:50,174
On Windows, file names are not case sensitive, so this pattern also catches files ending in lowercase dot j p g.

29
00:01:50,874 --> 00:01:51,679
Now the loop.

30
00:01:51,679 --> 00:01:58,555
enumerate gives us a counter alongside each file, and start equals one makes it count from one instead of zero.

31
00:01:58,555 --> 00:02:02,643
For each old file, with name builds a new path in the same folder.

32
00:02:02,643 --> 00:02:09,085
Inside the f string, i colon zero three d pads the number to three digits, so one becomes zero zero one.

33
00:02:09,085 --> 00:02:11,811
And suffix dot lower turns J P G into j p g.

34
00:02:12,511 --> 00:02:15,211
That zero padding matters more than it looks.

35
00:02:15,211 --> 00:02:19,231
Without it, your file explorer would sort trip ten before trip two.

36
00:02:19,231 --> 00:02:24,871
With three digits, everything lines up perfectly, all the way to nine hundred and ninety nine.

37
00:02:25,571 --> 00:02:28,609
Here's the most important habit in this whole video.

38
00:02:28,609 --> 00:02:30,829
Notice the last line is commented out.

39
00:02:30,829 --> 00:02:33,750
Right now the script only prints what it would do.

40
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:35,210
This is called a dry run.

41
00:02:35,210 --> 00:02:35,969
Let's run it.

42
00:02:36,669 --> 00:02:39,346
In the terminal, type python rename dot p y.

43
00:02:39,346 --> 00:02:40,320
And there it is.

44
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:42,936
Every old name, an arrow, and the new name.

45
00:02:42,936 --> 00:02:44,882
Nothing has changed on disk yet.

46
00:02:44,882 --> 00:02:49,627
If something looks wrong, you fix the script and run it again, with zero risk.

47
00:02:50,327 --> 00:02:51,790
Happy with the preview?

48
00:02:51,790 --> 00:02:56,752
Remove the hash symbol in front of old dot rename, so that line actually runs.

49
00:02:56,752 --> 00:02:59,296
Save the file, and run it one more time.

50
00:02:59,996 --> 00:03:03,098
Same output as before, but this time it's real.

51
00:03:03,098 --> 00:03:07,322
List the folder, and every photo now has a clean, numbered name.

52
00:03:07,322 --> 00:03:10,489
And notes dot t x t is exactly where we left it.

53
00:03:10,489 --> 00:03:15,109
Six files here, but it would be the same ten seconds for six thousand.

54
00:03:15,809 --> 00:03:17,016
Before and after.

55
00:03:17,016 --> 00:03:20,639
Seven files, one command, and nothing done by hand.

56
00:03:21,339 --> 00:03:24,492
If it didn't work, it's almost always one of three things.

57
00:03:24,492 --> 00:03:30,147
If nothing prints at all, the pattern didn't match anything, so check the folder name and the extension.

58
00:03:30,147 --> 00:03:36,073
If you see python is not recognized, or command not found, try typing python three instead, or py on Windows.

59
00:03:36,073 --> 00:03:42,489
And if you get file not found error, the terminal is open in the wrong folder, so go back and check with l s or d i r.

60
00:03:43,189 --> 00:03:45,651
And here's a question people always ask.

61
00:03:45,651 --> 00:03:46,697
Is there an undo?

62
00:03:46,697 --> 00:03:47,558
Not by itself.

63
00:03:47,558 --> 00:03:51,313
Once a file is renamed, Python doesn't remember the old name.

64
00:03:51,313 --> 00:03:57,405
That's exactly why we do the dry run first, and why a quick copy of the folder is your best friend.

65
00:03:58,105 --> 00:04:01,298
Two quick upgrades make this script safer for real life.

66
00:04:01,298 --> 00:04:05,174
First, sorting by name isn't always the order the photos were taken.

67
00:04:05,174 --> 00:04:12,642
Add key equals lambda, p dot stat, dot s t underscore m time, and the files are sorted by the time they were last modified instead.

68
00:04:12,642 --> 00:04:16,176
Second, before renaming, check if the new name already exists.

69
00:04:16,176 --> 00:04:19,767
If it does, skip it, so you never overwrite a file by accident.

70
00:04:20,467 --> 00:04:24,415
And because the new name is just an f string, you can make it anything you like.

71
00:04:24,415 --> 00:04:26,143
Want the year and a place in front?

72
00:04:26,143 --> 00:04:29,499
Write twenty twenty six, dash, lisbon, dash, then the padded number.

73
00:04:29,499 --> 00:04:31,176
Want to keep part of the old name?

74
00:04:31,176 --> 00:04:38,332
old dot stem gives you the name without the extension, so you could write trip dash, then old dot stem, and keep the camera number for reference.

75
00:04:39,032 --> 00:04:42,790
What if your folder mixes J P G, J P E G and P N G files?

76
00:04:42,790 --> 00:04:52,480
Instead of glob, use iterdir, which lists everything in the folder, and keep only the files whose lowercase suffix is in a small set of extensions.

77
00:04:52,480 --> 00:05:01,445
This one line works the same on every operating system, because we lower the suffix ourselves, so upper and lower case no longer matter.

78
00:05:01,445 --> 00:05:09,157
The rest of the loop stays exactly the same, except you keep each file's own extension with old dot suffix dot lower.

79
00:05:09,857 --> 00:05:10,679
One last tip.

80
00:05:10,679 --> 00:05:15,611
Before you run any script that changes files, make a copy of the folder first.

81
00:05:15,611 --> 00:05:19,658
It takes two seconds, and it turns any mistake into a non event.

82
00:05:20,358 --> 00:05:21,212
So, to recap.

83
00:05:21,212 --> 00:05:23,115
Path and glob find the files.

84
00:05:23,115 --> 00:05:26,135
enumerate and an f string build the new names.

85
00:05:26,135 --> 00:05:28,564
A dry run shows you the result first.

86
00:05:28,564 --> 00:05:30,927
And one line does the actual rename.

87
00:05:31,627 --> 00:05:39,467
Next on Shortcut Lab, we'll go one step further, and automatically sort a messy downloads folder into subfolders by file type.

88
00:05:39,467 --> 00:05:44,009
If this saved you some time, subscribe, and I'll see you in the next one.
